---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Equation from '../../../components/viz/Equation.astro';
import ScientificFigure from '../../../components/viz/ScientificFigure.astro';
import FieldExplorer from '../../../components/viz/FieldExplorer.astro';
import ThreeDField from '../../../components/viz/ThreeDField.astro';
import PubItem from '../../../components/ui/PubItem.astro';
import CitationMeta from '../../../components/ui/CitationMeta.astro';
import { byId, citation, bibtex } from '../../../data/publications';
import { url } from '../../../i18n/utils';
import fieldsFig from '../../../assets/figures/carbon2026-fields.png';
import compFig from '../../../assets/figures/carbon2026-composition.png';

const p = byId('littin2026carbon')!;
const related = ['littin2024sat', 'littin2025scatt', 'littin2026saf', 'yap2026dual'].map((id) => byId(id)!);
const credit = 'Figure regenerated for this site from the author’s data. Source: Littin et al., Carbon 250 (2026) 121296, CC BY 4.0.';
const jsonld = [{
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'ScholarlyArticle',
  headline: p.title,
  author: p.authors.map((name) => ({ '@type': 'Person', name })),
  datePublished: '2026-01-22',
  isPartOf: { '@type': 'Periodical', name: 'Carbon' },
  identifier: { '@type': 'PropertyValue', propertyID: 'DOI', value: p.doi },
  sameAs: `https://doi.org/${p.doi}`,
  license: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/',
}];
---
<Base title="Physics-informed retrieval of soot properties (Carbon 2026)" math ogType="article" jsonld={jsonld}
  description="Explainer for Littin et al., Carbon 2026: a physics-informed neural network jointly inverts attenuation, emission and multi-angle scattering measurements to retrieve temperature, volume fraction, aggregate size and composition fields.">
  <Fragment slot="head"><CitationMeta pub={p} /></Fragment>

  <header class="page-head frame">
    <p class="eyebrow"><a href={url('/publications/')}>Publications</a> · Case study · Carbon 2026</p>
    <h1 class="title">Three instruments, one physical model, one network</h1>
    <p class="lede abstract">Can several optical measurements of the same object be inverted together, so that the recovered fields agree with all of them and with the physics that links them?</p>
    <p class="meta cite">{citation(p)} <span class="tag tag--established">Open access · CC BY 4.0</span></p>
    <ul class="links head-links">
      <li><a href={`https://doi.org/${p.doi}`}>Read the paper (DOI)</a></li>
      <li><a href={url('/talks/carbon-2026/')}>Interactive presentation</a></li>
      <li><a href="#resources">Code and data</a></li>
      <li><a href={url(`/publications/bibtex/${p.id}.bib`)} download>BibTeX</a></li>
    </ul>
  </header>

  <section class="section frame">
    <div class="row">
      <h2 class="row__label">In thirty seconds</h2>
      <div class="prose short">
        <p>Light shone through a flame and light emitted or scattered by it depend on the same hidden quantities: how much soot is present, how hot it is, how large its aggregates are and what it is made of. Each measurement is usually inverted on its own, with its own calibration, and the answers do not quite agree.</p>
        <p>In this work a single neural network takes a position <em>(r, z)</em> in the flame and returns all the hidden quantities there. It is not trained on labels, because none exist. It is trained so that, when its outputs are passed through light-scattering theory and Planck’s law, they reproduce every measurement at every wavelength at the same time. The instrument calibration factors are solved for along the way.</p>
        <p>Nothing in the formulation is specific to flames, so the same construction could be tried wherever several instruments observe one state through known physics. The paper itself demonstrates it on one axisymmetric laminar flame.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section frame">
    <ScientificFigure src={fieldsFig} number={1} alt="Three maps of a laminar flame cross-section between 20 and 38 mm above the burner: soot volume fraction peaking near the wings at about 7.8 ppm, temperature between 1710 and 1880 K with higher values on the wings, and graphitic fraction rising towards the upper wings."
      caption="Fields retrieved by the network in a laminar ethylene flame, mirrored about the axis: (a) soot volume fraction <em>f<sub>s</sub></em>, (b) soot temperature <em>T</em>, (c) graphitic fraction <em>f<sub>G</sub></em>. White regions are where not all diagnostics have signal, so no retrieval is attempted."
      credit={credit} />
  </section>

  <section class="section frame">
    <div class="row">
      <h2 class="row__label">Why separate inversions disagree</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Three techniques were applied to an axisymmetric ethylene flame at five wavelengths between 405 and 750 nm. Line-of-sight attenuation (LOSA) measures extinction in absolute terms. Spectral soot emission (SSE) measures radiated light, which depends on temperature. Multi-angle light scattering (MALS) is sensitive to aggregate size. Each camera image is first converted into local coefficients by Abel inversion with the spline-based method (SAT).</p>
        <p>The local coefficients are related by the Rayleigh–Debye–Gans theory for fractal aggregates:</p>
        <Equation tex={String.raw`\kappa_{abs}(\lambda) = \frac{6\pi f_s\, E(m)}{\lambda}, \qquad \kappa_{vv}(\lambda,0^\circ) = \frac{9\pi^2}{\lambda^4}\, f_s\, \frac{\overline{V_{agg}^2}}{\overline{V_{agg}}}\, F(m)`} />
        <Equation tex={String.raw`\kappa_{ext} = \kappa_{abs} + \kappa_{sca}, \qquad J(\lambda) = \kappa_{abs}(\lambda)\, B(T,\lambda)`} />
        <p>Here <em>f<sub>s</sub></em> is the soot volume fraction, <em>E(m)</em> and <em>F(m)</em> are absorption and scattering functions of the refractive index <em>m</em>, and <em>B</em> is the Planck function. Forward scattering <em>κ<sub>vv</sub>(0°)</em> also carries the aggregate volume moment; the angular dependence of scattering, integrated over angles, gives <em>κ<sub>sca</sub></em>. When absorption is deduced from emission and scattering from MALS independently, their sum does not match the extinction measured by LOSA. The errors of one diagnostic propagate into the next. There are about fifteen measured quantities for about eight unknown fields, and they are not mutually consistent.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section frame">
    <div class="row">
      <h2 class="row__label">Method: from images to fields</h2>
      <div>
        <ol class="pipe" aria-label="Processing chain">
          <li><strong>Measure.</strong> LOSA, SSE and MALS at five wavelengths and five angles; line-of-sight images.</li>
          <li><strong>Reconstruct.</strong> Abel inversion with trapping correction (SAT) gives local <em>κ<sub>ext</sub></em>, <em>κ<sub>vv</sub></em> and <em>J</em>.</li>
          <li><strong>Represent.</strong> A network maps <em>(r, z)</em> to <em>f<sub>s</sub>, T, V<sub>agg</sub>, f<sub>O</sub>, f<sub>G</sub>, f<sub>A</sub></em> and calibration gains <em>γ</em>.</li>
          <li><strong>Predict.</strong> RDG-FA and Planck’s law turn those outputs into predicted coefficients.</li>
          <li><strong>Reconcile.</strong> The misfit to all measurements is minimised at once.</li>
        </ol>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>The network has ten fully connected layers of twenty neurons with tanh activations. Its outputs are bounded by construction: a softmax keeps the organic, graphitic and amorphous fractions positive with unit sum, and sigmoids keep <em>f<sub>s</sub></em>, <em>T</em> and aggregate volume in physical ranges. The refractive index is not a free output. It follows from the composition through the dispersion model of Bescond et al., replaced during training by a small surrogate network that reproduces it within 0.1%.</p>
          <p>The loss is a sum of normalised misfits, one per measured quantity, with the absolute LOSA extinction fixing the overall scale:</p>
          <Equation tex={String.raw`\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_{ext} + \mathcal{L}_{\kappa_{vv}(0^\circ)} + \mathcal{L}_{sca} + \mathcal{L}_{J}, \qquad \mathcal{L}_X = \frac{1}{N_\lambda N_p}\sum_{i=1}^{N_\lambda}\sum_{j=1}^{N_p} \frac{\big(X^{pred}_{i,j} - X^{meas}_{i,j}\big)^2}{\big(\max_j |X^{meas}_{i,j}|\big)^2}`} />
          <p>Because emission and scattering must add up to the absolute extinction at every wavelength, the wavelength-dependent gains <em>γ<sub>J</sub><sup>λ</sup></em> and <em>γ<sub>s</sub><sup>λ</sup></em> of the emission and scattering channels are determined by the data. This is what makes the method calibration-free. Training uses Adam with a learning rate of 10<sup>−4</sup> and takes about six minutes on one GPU.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section frame">
    <div class="row">
      <h2 class="row__label">The retrieved fields</h2>
      <div>
        <p class="prose">Choose a field and move over the map to read values. The data are the author’s network outputs behind the published figures, downsampled for the web.</p>
        <FieldExplorer base="/data/carbon2026/" id="c26" fields={[
          { key: 'fs', label: 'Volume fraction' },
          { key: 'T', label: 'Temperature' },
          { key: 'fO', label: 'Organic' },
          { key: 'fA', label: 'Amorphous' },
          { key: 'fG', label: 'Graphitic' },
          { key: 'V2', label: 'Aggregate volume' },
          { key: 'Em532', label: 'E(m), 532 nm' },
          { key: 'Em1064', label: 'E(m), 1064 nm' },
        ]} caption="<span class='fig-num'>Figure 2.</span>Interactive view of the retrieved fields (half-plane data mirrored about the axis). Units: ppm for volume fraction, K for temperature, 10<sup>6</sup>&nbsp;nm<sup>3</sup> for the aggregate volume moment ⟨V<sup>2</sup>⟩/⟨V⟩; composition fractions sum to one; E(m) is the absorption function of the soot refractive index." />
        <h3 class="h3">What the camera actually sees</h3>
        <p class="prose">The reconstructed field can be revolved around the axis and observed again. At zero elevation the image below is the line-of-sight integral that a side-on camera records, which is what step 2 has to undo. Tilting the view shows the hollow, annular structure hidden in the projection.</p>
        <ThreeDField base="/data/carbon2026/" field="fs" id="c26td" caption="<span class='fig-num'>Figure 3.</span>Line-of-sight integral of the revolved soot volume fraction, computed in the browser. Only heights where the full radial profile was retrieved are shown." />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section frame">
    <div class="row">
      <h2 class="row__label">Results</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Validation.</strong> On synthetic flames generated with the CoFlame code, the network recovered temperature, volume fraction and composition with mean relative errors of about 4 to 6% without noise. Errors stayed bounded and localised with multiplicative noise and with parts of the data removed.</li>
          <li><strong>Consistency.</strong> On the experimental flame, extinction reconstructed from emission and scattering now matches the measured extinction, with the largest deviation (about 5%) at 480 nm. The inferred gains were 0.65 to 0.8 for scattering and 0.73 to 1.28 for emission.</li>
          <li><strong>Agreement with the literature.</strong> Radial profiles of volume fraction and temperature at 30 mm above the burner are consistent with published data, and the temperatures agree with two-colour pyrometry and CARS measurements reported in the literature.</li>
          <li><strong>A new quantity.</strong> Adding scattering to the inversion makes the amorphous fraction observable. The maps show a transition from organic soot near the base, to amorphous along the centre, to graphitic on the hot wings.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <ScientificFigure src={compFig} number={4} alt="Ternary colour map of soot composition: blue (organic) near the bottom centre, reddish (amorphous) along the centre, and green (graphitic) along the outer wings."
      caption="Composition as a ternary colour map, with the paper’s convention: blue organic, green graphitic, red amorphous." credit={credit} sizes="(min-width: 40rem) 30rem, 100vw" />
  </section>

  <section class="section frame">
    <div class="row">
      <h2 class="row__label">Why it matters, and limitations</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>When diagnostics are analysed separately, their results are combined afterwards and inconsistencies have to be explained case by case. Writing the shared physics into a single loss turns reconciliation into an optimisation problem with a clear objective, and it lets the data correct the calibration instead of the other way round. For combustion this gives maps of soot optical index and composition that were not available before. For inverse problems more generally it is an example of inference without labels, where the physical model takes the place of a training set.</p>
        <p>The limitations matter too. RDG-FA is an approximation for these aggregates, the composition fractions are an effective description rather than a chemical analysis, and the paper does not yet quantify uncertainty in the retrieved fields. Which wavelengths are really needed is also left as an open question. These points motivate the <a href={url('/research/#uncertainty')}>uncertainty and identifiability</a> line of the research programme.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section frame" id="resources">
    <div class="row">
      <h2 class="row__label">Resources</h2>
      <div>
        <table class="res">
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Paper</th><td><a href={`https://doi.org/${p.doi}`}>doi:{p.doi}</a> (open access, CC BY 4.0). Preprint: <a href="https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.5726644">SSRN 5726644</a>.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Slides</th><td><a href={url('/talks/carbon-2026/')}>Interactive HTML presentation</a> (about 15 minutes; press <kbd>S</kbd> for speaker view).</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Reconstruction code</th><td><a href={url('/software/pysat-abel/')}>pysat-abel</a>, the spline-based Abel transform used in step 2.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">PINN code</th><td>Available upon request.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Data on this page</th><td>Network outputs on a 160 × 100 grid (<a href={url('/data/carbon2026/meta.json')}>metadata</a>), derived from the published results and shared under CC BY 4.0.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <details class="bib">
          <summary class="meta">BibTeX</summary>
          <pre><code>{bibtex(p)}</code></pre>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section frame">
    <div class="row">
      <h2 class="row__label">Related publications</h2>
      <div>{related.map((r) => <PubItem pub={r} compact headingLevel="h3" />)}</div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

